{"prompt": {"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant that repairs Java 8 code so that it correctly solves the given programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Repair the following Java 8 code so that it correctly solves the programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console. Your repaired code should implement the problem correctly, meaning that it should produce the correct output for any valid input.\nPrint your repaired code and also the confidence score you give to your repair. The confidence score should be a number between 0 and 1. The format of the output should be:\n<repaired_code>\n<your_repaired_code>\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n<your_confidence_score>\n</confidence>\nDo not provide any other text or markdown fences.\n\nHere is the problem description:\n<problem_description>\nLittle Johnny Bubbles enjoys spending hours in front of his computer playing video games. His favorite game is Bubble Strike, fast-paced bubble shooting online game for two players.Each game is set in one of the N maps, each having different terrain configuration. First phase of each game decides on which map the game will be played. The game system randomly selects three maps and shows them to the players. Each player must pick one of those three maps to be discarded. The game system then randomly selects one of the maps that were not picked by any of the players and starts the game.Johnny is deeply enthusiastic about the game and wants to spend some time studying maps, thus increasing chances to win games played on those maps. However, he also needs to do his homework, so he does not have time to study all the maps. That is why he asked himself the following question: \"What is the minimum number of maps I have to study, so that the probability to play one of those maps is at least $$$P$$$\"?Can you help Johnny find the answer for this question? You can assume Johnny's opponents do not know him, and they will randomly pick maps.\n</problem_description>\n\nHere is the Java 8 code you have to repair:\n<original_code>\nimport java.io.*;\nimport java.util.*;\n\npublic class bubblestrike {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {\n        BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(System.in));\n        String line = reader.readLine();\n        int idx = line.indexOf(' ');\n        int x = Integer.parseInt(line.substring(0, idx));\n        double prob = Double.parseDouble(line.substring(idx + 1));\n        int pos = x;\n        for (int step = x; step >= 1; step /= 2) {\n            while (evaluate(pos - step, x - (pos - step), prob)) {\n                pos -= step;\n            }\n        }\n        System.out.println(pos);\n    }\n\n    private static boolean evaluate(int good, int bad, double target) {\n        double g = good;\n        double b = bad;\n        double n = g + b;\n        double numerator = (b * (b - 1) / 4.0) + \n                          (g * (g - 1) * (g - 2) / 6.0) + \n                          (b * g * (g - 1) / 2.0);\n        double denominator = n * (n - 1) * (n - 2) / 6.0;\n        return numerator / denominator >= target;\n    }\n}\n</original_code>\n"}], "temp": 0, "sample_size": 1, "logprobs": true, "model": "qwen/qwen3-coder-next"}, "response": {"samples": [{"content": "<repaired_code>\nimport java.io.*;\nimport java.util.*;\n\npublic class bubblestrike {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {\n        BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(System.in));\n        String line = reader.readLine();\n        int idx = line.indexOf(' ');\n        int n = Integer.parseInt(line.substring(0, idx));\n        double targetProb = Double.parseDouble(line.substring(idx + 1));\n        \n        int lo = 0, hi = n;\n        while (lo < hi) {\n            int mid = (lo + hi) / 2;\n            if (canWinWith(mid, n, targetProb)) {\n                hi = mid;\n            } else {\n                lo = mid + 1;\n            }\n        }\n        System.out.println(lo);\n    }\n\n    private static boolean canWinWith(int good, int total, double target) {\n        int bad = total - good;\n        if (good == 0) return false;\n        \n        // Total ways to choose 3 maps from total\n        long totalWays = (long)total * (total - 1) * (total - 2) / 6;\n        \n        // Count favorable outcomes where at least one player picks a good map\n        // and the final map is good\n        \n        // Case 1: Both players pick the same good map\n        // System then randomly picks one of the remaining maps (2 maps left)\n        // For the game to be on a good map, one of the remaining maps must be good\n        long case1 = (long)good * 1; // choose which good map both pick\n        \n        // Case 2: Both players pick the same bad map\n        // System then randomly picks one of the remaining maps\n        // For game to be on good map, at least one of remaining maps is good\n        long case2 = (long)bad * 1;\n        \n        // Case 3: Players pick different maps\n        // Subcase 3a: Both pick good maps (different ones)\n        long case3a = (long)good * (good - 1) / 2; // choose 2 different good maps\n        \n        // Subcase 3b: One picks good, one picks bad\n        long case3b = (long)good * bad;\n        \n        // Subcase 3c: Both pick bad maps (different ones)\n        long case3c = (long)bad * (bad - 1) / 2;\n        \n        // For each case, calculate probability that final map is good\n        \n        // Case 1: Both pick same good map\n        // Remaining maps: total-1 maps, with good-1 good maps left\n        // System randomly picks one of remaining maps\n        // Probability final map is good = (good-1)/(total-1)\n        long favorable1 = case1 * (good - 1);\n        \n        // Case 2: Both pick same bad map\n        // Remaining maps: total-1 maps, with good good maps\n        // Probability final map is good = good/(total-1)\n        long favorable2 = case2 * good;\n        \n        // Case 3a: Both pick different good maps\n        // Remaining maps: total-2 maps, with good-2 good maps left\n        // System randomly picks one of remaining maps\n        // Probability final map is good = (good-2)/(total-2)\n        long favorable3a = case3a * Math.max(0, good - 2);\n        \n        // Case 3b: One picks good, one picks bad\n        // Remaining maps: total-2 maps, with good-1 good maps left\n        // Probability final map is good = (good-1)/(total-2)\n        long favorable3b = case3b * Math.max(0, good - 1);\n        \n        // Case 3c: Both pick different bad maps\n        // Remaining maps: total-2 maps, with good good maps\n        // Probability final map is good = good/(total-2)\n        long favorable3c = case3c * good;\n        \n        long totalFavorable = favorable1 + favorable2 + favorable3a + favorable3b + favorable3c;\n        \n        double probability = (double)totalFavorable / totalWays;\n        return probability >= target;\n    }\n}\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n0.85\n</confidence>", "token_logprobs": [-3.576278118089249e-07, -0.00014745102089364082, -4.6491513785440475e-06, 0.0, -1.4543427823809907e-05, -9.65590606938349e-06, -4.768370445162873e-07, -4.0531076592742465e-06, -8.22540732769994e-06, -5.602820692729438e-06, -1.0728830375228426e-06, -3.2543604902457446e-05, -2.3841830625315197e-06, 0.0, -2.145764938177308e-06, -7.855583680793643e-05, -2.3841830625315197e-06, 0.0, -1.1920928244535389e-07, -8.344646857949556e-07, -2.0265558760002023e-06, -3.2186455882765586e-06, -4.172316494077677e-06, -1.680836794548668e-05, -3.933898824470816e-06, -9.775113539944869e-06, -3.6477376852417365e-05, -1.156323378381785e-05, 0.0, -4.351044481154531e-05, -1.5616295058862306e-05, -6.9141146923357155e-06, 0.0, -5.960462772236497e-07, -1.2278481335670222e-05, -1.0847986231965479e-05, -5.4